## Chart Details

This chart will do the following:

* Rollout Victoria Metrics Single.

{{ include "chart.installSection" . }}

{{ include "chart.uninstallSection" . }}

### HTTP listen address

The chart configures the HTTP listen address via `.Values.server.http` list.
Each entry in the list configures a separate HTTP listener.
The primary listener (marked with `primary: true`) is used for Pod health checks and Service port generation.

To enable TLS on the HTTP listener:

```yaml
server:
  http:
    - name: https
      primary: true
      value: :8428
      tls: true
      tlsCertFile: /path/to/tls.crt
      tlsKeyFile: /path/to/tls.key
```

To expose an additional listener on a different port:

```yaml
server:
  http:
    - name: http
      primary: true
      value: :8428
    - name: https
      value: :8429
      tls: true
      tlsCertFile: /path/to/tls.crt
      tlsKeyFile: /path/to/tls.key
```

## Resource Naming

By default, this chart uses release-name based resource naming for historical reasons and
backwards compatibility with existing deployments.

> [!WARNING]
> For new deployments, it is recommended to set `useLegacyNaming: false` to use operator-style
> names (e.g. `vmsingle-<release>`). This aligns resource names with the
> [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) convention and
> simplifies future migration to operator-managed resources.

### VictoriaMetrics operator

With `operator.enabled: true` the chart renders a [VMSingle](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/resources/vmsingle/) resource
instead of its own Deployment/StatefulSet, Service, ServiceAccount and scrape/relabel ConfigMaps,
and the [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) manages the workload.
The operator must already be installed in the cluster. Operator mode always uses operator-style names, regardless of `useLegacyNaming`.

The VMSingle is named after the release (or `fullnameOverride`), so the operator creates `vmsingle-<release>` objects.
Ingress and HTTPRoute are still rendered by the chart and target them. The operator runs the VMSingle as a single pod, so `podDisruptionBudget.enabled` and `server.replicaCount` greater than 1 fail rendering. Instead of rendering its own scrape object, the chart lets the operator create the VMServiceScrape when `server.serviceMonitor.enabled` is enabled, and disables it otherwise.

The spec is built from the `server` values: image, replicas, resources, scheduling, env, extra args, volumes, containers,
pod metadata, security context, HTTP listen port, retention period, Service settings, service account and license.
In addition:

- `networkPolicy` ingress and egress rules become the resource `networkPolicy`, and the operator creates the NetworkPolicy. Its labels and annotations can't be set;
- the chart keeps managing the `server.persistentVolume` claim (or uses `existingClaim`) and passes it to the VMSingle as its data volume,
  mounted at `server.persistentVolume.mountPath`. A release that ran with a chart-managed claim keeps its data after switching to the operator;
- `server.relabel` becomes `inlineRelabelConfig`, or `relabelConfig` for `server.relabel.configMap`;
- `server.vmbackupmanager` becomes `vmBackup`. Unlike the chart, the operator doesn't append the pod name to `destination`;
- `server.scrape.config.scrape_configs` and `server.scrape.extraScrapeConfigs` jobs become [VMScrapeConfig](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/resources/vmscrapeconfig/)
  resources selected by the VMSingle, and `scrape_interval`, `scrape_timeout` and `external_labels` of `server.scrape.config.global`
  become VMSingle fields. Each job keeps its `job` label.

Rendering fails for settings without an operator equivalent: `server.scrape.configMap`, other `global` scrape settings,
and scrape job options such as inline credentials (`basic_auth`, `bearer_token`) or service discovery types other than
Kubernetes, static, file, DNS and HTTP. Create the corresponding operator resources yourself in that case.

Fields that have no chart value, or need a different value, go to `operator.spec`, which is deep-merged over the generated spec:

```yaml
operator:
  enabled: true
  spec:
    logLevel: WARN
    useStrictSecurity: true
    extraArgs:
      search.maxUniqueTimeseries: "1000000"
```

Probes keep the operator defaults, and the HTTP port follows the primary `server.http` listener. Switching a release to `operator.enabled: true` recreates its pod.

